      "title": "13.11.1 Chlorine addition to water",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "b07b1f6c-f5a4-509d-ae86-73be5bffb0d4",
          "pdf_from": 301,
          "pdf_to": 301,
          "note": "Topic 13.11.1, printed page 301."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "When chlorine gas (Cl₂) is added to water, it undergoes hydrolysis to form a mixture of hydrochloric acid (HCl) and chloric (I) acid (HClO)."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{Cl2_{(g)} + H2O_{(l)} -> HCl_{(aq)} + HClO_{(aq)}}",
            "text_alt": "Cl₂(g) + H₂O(l) → HCl(aq) + HClO(aq)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Chloric (I) acid (HOCl) is a weak acid and partially dissociate in water to form hydrogen ions (H⁺) and chlorate (I) ion (OCl⁻)."
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
